  "body": "Add a guide section on workholding and fixtures. It's one of the hardest parts of real machining, and CamCraft has real features for it (fixtures, the fixture library, workholding rpm limits, holder and fixture collision checks) that the guide barely covers.\n\nCover:\n- **Why it matters in CamCraft:** fixtures and workholding are part of the collision and gouge checks; a tool or holder that hits a vise jaw or clamp is refused, so modelling the workholding is how a user gets a trustworthy verify.\n- **Milling workholding:** vises (and soft jaws), fixture plates and subplates, clamps and toe clamps, modular fixturing; how to add each to a setup, place it, and keep it in the fixture library for reuse.\n- **Vendor STEP fixtures:** link and summarise `docs/user-guide/vendor-fixtures.md` (units check, orientation, placement, saving to the library).\n- **Turning workholding:** chucks and jaws, collets, tailstock and centres; the chuck's maximum rpm and how CamCraft holds turning and live-tool operations to it.\n- **Router and plasma:** vacuum tables, spoilboards, screws and tabs, slats; what can be modelled and what can't.\n- **Second setups:** flipping the part, carrying stock from setup 1 to setup 2, re-establishing the WCS, and soft jaws or a fixture for op 2.\n- **Common mistakes:** stick-out too short for the vise depth, a clamp in the toolpath, the WCS set on the vise instead of the part, an rpm over the chuck's limit. For each, the refusal you'd see and the fix.\n\nDone means: a workholding and fixtures section in the guide covering mill, lathe, router/plasma, vendor STEP fixtures and second setups, with the refusals each one produces, linked from setups.md and the machine types section.\n",
